From: Kernel audit <littleddfu@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] sctp: do not roll back stream->outcnt on a replayed reconf response

sctp_process_strreset_resp() resolves an incoming Re-configuration Response
with sctp_chunk_lookup_strreset_param(), which walks the parameters of our
own retained asoc->strreset_chunk and returns the first whose request_seq
matches the response's response_seq.  It keeps no record of which request
sequence numbers have already been answered, and the request chunk is only
released once asoc->strreset_outstanding reaches zero:

	asoc->strreset_outstanding--;
	asoc->strreset_outseq++;
	if (!asoc->strreset_outstanding) {
		...
		sctp_chunk_put(asoc->strreset_chunk);
		asoc->strreset_chunk = NULL;
	}

sctp_make_strreset_addstrm() emits the add-out and add-in parameters with
request_seq S and S+1 and leaves strreset_outstanding at 2, so a peer that
answers twice with response_seq S has both responses resolve to the same
add-out parameter.  The add-out handler then computes

	nums   = ntohs(addstrm->number_of_streams);
	number = stream->outcnt - nums;

with both operands __u16.  The first response rolls stream->outcnt back
correctly; the second subtracts the same count from the already
rolled-back value and wraps.  With 10 outbound streams and an add of 100,
outcnt goes 10 -> 110 at request time, back to 10, and then to 65446.

stream->outcnt is a loop bound and an index gate.  SCTP_SO() is
genradix_ptr(), which returns NULL for an index whose node was never
allocated, so sctp_stream_free() walks off the end of the populated
genradix on teardown:

  Oops: general protection fault, probably for non-canonical address
        0xdffffc0000000001
  KASAN: null-ptr-deref in range [0x0000000000000008-0x000000000000000f]
  RIP: 0010:sctp_stream_free_ext+0x61/0x120
  Call Trace:
   sctp_stream_free+0x6f/0xd0
   sctp_association_free+0x21c/0x660
   sctp_do_sm+0x1edb/0x4720
   sctp_primitive_ABORT+0x8a/0xc0
   sctp_close+0x17e/0x600
   __x64_sys_close+0x7b/0xc0

sctp_stream_clear() and the sinfo_stream bound in sctp_sendmsg_to_asoc()
are affected the same way.

Only apply the rollback when the subtraction is valid.  Note the
SCTP_STRRESET_PERFORMED arm is already safe: its loop starts at the
wrapped value, which is greater than outcnt, so it simply does not run.

A deeper fix would be to reject a response whose response_seq is not the
one asoc->strreset_outseq expects, making every response single use.  That
changes which responses are accepted on the wire, so it is left out here.

diff --git a/net/sctp/stream.c b/net/sctp/stream.c
index 34ffe6c945a4..33038012b65d 100644
--- a/net/sctp/stream.c
+++ b/net/sctp/stream.c
@@ -1052,7 +1052,21 @@ struct sctp_chunk *sctp_process_strreset_resp(
 		if (result == SCTP_STRRESET_PERFORMED) {
 			for (i = number; i < stream->outcnt; i++)
 				SCTP_SO(stream, i)->state = SCTP_STREAM_OPEN;
-		} else {
+		} else if (nums <= stream->outcnt) {
+			/* Only roll back when the subtraction is valid.
+			 *
+			 * sctp_chunk_lookup_strreset_param() resolves a
+			 * response by request_seq alone and keeps no record of
+			 * which requests have already been answered, while the
+			 * request chunk is retained until strreset_outstanding
+			 * reaches zero.  A peer that sends two responses
+			 * carrying the same response_seq therefore resolves the
+			 * same add-out request twice, and the second
+			 * subtraction runs against an outcnt that has already
+			 * been rolled back - wrapping the u16 and leaving
+			 * stream->outcnt far beyond the number of allocated
+			 * stream slots.
+			 */
 			sched = sctp_sched_ops_from_stream(stream);
 			sched->unsched_all(stream);
 			sctp_stream_outq_migrate(stream, NULL, number);
